24	To capture phase variation, the per-problem \emph{amplification ratio} is:
25	\begin{equation}
26	    G^{(i)} = \frac{\mathrm{II}^{(i)}_{\,\textit{low}}}{\mathrm{II}^{(i)}_{\,\textit{high}}},
27	    \label{eq:gradient}
28	\end{equation}
29	where $\mathrm{II}^{(i)}_{\,\textit{low/high}}$ is the interaction index in the low/high-sparsity phase for problem $i$. We report $G = \widetilde{G^{(i)}}$, the median across problems, with bootstrap CIs. $G \gg 1$ indicates phase-concentrated severity.
30	
31	\paragraph{Phase segmentation.}
32	\label{sec:phase-segmentation}
33	Inspired by ThinKV's phase-aware perspective on reasoning-time compression~\cite{thinkv-iclr2026}, each generated sequence is partitioned into three phases (low-, mid-, high-sparsity) by tercile boundaries of per-position attention sparsity, using a quantile-based segmentation rather than explicit reasoning delimiters (e.g., \texttt{<think>} tokens). Low-sparsity phases correspond to broadly distributed attention (exploratory reasoning); high-sparsity phases to concentrated attention (structured computation). Details in Appendix~\ref{app:formulation-details}.
